# 35. VOLATILITY

**Volatility** is a memory-forensics framework.

It is used to analyze memory dumps.

Concept:

```text
RAM Image
    ↓
Volatility
    ↓
Processes
Connections
Modules
Memory artifacts
```

---

# 36. WHY MEMORY FORENSICS?

A disk investigation may show:

```text
Suspicious.exe exists
```

But memory may reveal:

```text
Suspicious.exe
   ↓
Currently running
   ↓
Process information
   ↓
Network connection
```

Memory can therefore provide valuable runtime evidence.

---

# 37. VOLATILITY 3

Modern Volatility workflows commonly use **Volatility 3**.

General syntax:

```bash
python vol.py -f memory.raw <plugin>
```

The exact plugin name and supported artifacts depend on the Volatility version and memory image.

---

# 38. IMPORTANT VOLATILITY CONCEPT

Volatility does not magically analyze every memory dump.

Successful analysis depends on:

- Correct memory image
- Supported operating system
- Appropriate symbols/plugins
- Correct plugin selection
- Image quality

---

# 39. PROCESS ANALYSIS

A key memory-forensics task is identifying processes.

Concept:

```text
Memory
  ↓
Process enumeration
  ↓
PID
Name
Parent
Path
Threads
```

Questions:

- Which processes were running?
- Which parent created them?
- Is the process unusual?
- Does its path make sense?

---

# 40. PROCESS TREE

A process tree can be extremely useful.

Example:

```text
explorer.exe
    ↓
browser.exe
    ↓
document.exe
    ↓
powershell.exe
```

A suspicious parent-child relationship may deserve investigation.

Important:

> A suspicious process tree is an indicator, not automatic proof of malware.

---

# 41. VOLATILITY NETWORK ANALYSIS

Memory can contain evidence of network connections.

Investigators may examine:

- Local addresses
- Remote addresses
- Ports
- Process association

Concept:

```text
Process
   ↓
Network Connection
   ↓
Remote IP:Port
```

This can help correlate malware activity.

---

# 42. MEMORY MODULE ANALYSIS

Memory analysis may identify loaded modules.

Questions:

- Which DLLs/modules are loaded?
- Are any unexpected?
- Do paths make sense?
- Are there suspicious modules associated with processes?

---

# 43. COMMAND-LINE FORENSICS

Process command-line information can reveal how a process was launched.

Example concept:

```text
Process
 ↓
Command line
 ↓
Arguments
```

This can help investigators understand execution.

---

# 44. MALWARE IN MEMORY

Malware may leave artifacts in RAM even if:

- Its file was deleted
- Its disk presence is obscured
- It exists only temporarily
- It injected code into another process

Therefore:

> **Memory forensics can complement disk forensics.**

